  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 134 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nBelow normal temperatures dominate the forecast next week. Following \nSaturday night's front, high temperatures for most locations in the \nPermian Basin and southeast New Mexico top out in the mid 50s. \nMeanwhile, highs for locations closer to the Rio Grande will be in \nthe 60s and even low 70s. Continued cold air advection keeps lows in \nthe 30s and low 40s Monday morning, and highs in the 50s for most of \nthe area Monday afternoon. In fact, Monday morning portions of \nGaines and Dawson counties, along with parts of the Davis Mountains, \nmay be flirting with freezing temperatures for the first time this \nseason (10-30% chance of this occurring). By Tuesday, a shortwave \ntrough nears west Texas and southeast New Mexico. This system sends \nanother front southward into our area Tuesday afternoon and evening. \nAs a result, highs Tuesday and Wednesday remain in the 50s for most \nof the region (upper 40s in the far northern Permian Basin and in \nthe higher terrain, 60s along the Rio Grande). Low temperatures may \nonce again approach freezing for the northern Permian Basin, parts \nof Lea County, and in portions of the Davis and Guadalupe Mountains \n(25-50% chance of occurrence this time around for these locations). \n\nBy Thanksgiving Day, winds become southeasterly to southerly again, \nallowing temperatures to tick up a degree or two. Nevertheless, \nhighs remain about 5-10 degrees below normal for both Thanksgiving \nDay and Black Friday. Aside from a low chance (around 10%) of cold \nrain glancing our far northern counties Monday night into Tuesday \nmorning, most locations remain dry through at least Thanksgiving. By \nFriday into next weekend, some guidance is still trying to bring low \nrain chances across portions of the area. However, at this time \nconfidence remains low (10-15% chances), and details will become \nclearer over the next few forecast updates.\n\nSprang",
